"""Waybar widget for Lhassa: prints Waybar JSON objects, opens the dashboard, shows a menu.

    lhassa-waybar [status]        icon + short counter + tooltip (the group leader)
    lhassa-waybar agents          the Agents drawer module (active sessions per host)
    lhassa-waybar attention       the Attention drawer module (empty text = hidden)
    lhassa-waybar system          the System drawer module (profile, temperature, CPU, battery)
    lhassa-waybar open [section]  open the dashboard (default: approvals, or fleet when only a
                                  check or a service is failing; for "on-click")
    lhassa-waybar menu            right-click menu through wofi (summary, approve/deny,
                                  dashboard section, power profile)
    lhassa-waybar scroll up|down  cycle what the counter next to the icon shows

It only READS the state file that the running Lhassa process keeps fresh (see
lhassa/views/waybar_state.py and doc/waybar.md); it never imports Lhassa and never
touches the network. The only things it changes are its own counter-mode file
(next to the state file, mode 0600) and what the menu asks Lhassa to do, always
through the ``lhassa`` command (``approve``, ``deny``, ``action run``), never
by touching a database. It starts in a few ms and degrades to a calm "no brain"
when Lhassa is down. A v1 state file (no agents or attention sections) still renders.

Classes: ok, warn (something needs you), alert (a health check at alert level, a critical
review finding, a failed service, budget exhausted or a hot package), idle (no state
file), stale (state file too old, so Lhassa stopped updating it).
"""

